FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1645 — A `set` reuses any existing non-`Function` binding, including a `constant`: the filter is `existing.filter(|&binding| bindings[binding].kind != DefinitionKind::Function)`, so `constant x` followed by `set x to ...` binds the `set` name to the *constant* declaration instead of starting a new variable binding (no `mark_declaration`, no new reference set). This contradicts the report's stated invariant in the uncommitted REPORT diff ("a later `set`, `constant` or `to` replaces a binding of another kind") and the runtime, which refuses assignment to a constant entirely; goto on `set x` returns the `constant x` span and references merge the immutable constant with the attempted assignment. Fix: exclude `DefinitionKind::Constant` (and any other non-reusable kind) from the reuse filter, route it through `declare_replacing_function`/a fresh binding, and add a `constant` → `set` test mirroring `edge_constant_after_a_same_named_set_is_its_own_binding`. VERIFIED: traced lines 1637–1648; `existing` is `Some(constant)` and the `kind != Function` filter passes it through unchanged.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:1047 — `scope_function_binding` scans `binding_scopes` with `find` and returns *any* same-scope `Function` binding with the name, ignoring declaration order. After `to f(); set f to 1`, the scope entry is the replacement `Variable`, so a later `f()` call falls into this scan and is attributed to the shadowed-out function. The report itself states the runtime raises `RuntimeError: Unknown function 'f'` for such a call, yet navigation links it into the old function's reference set; `edge_later_set_shadows_an_earlier_function` tests only `say f`, never `say f()`, so the gap is unpinned. Fix: when the scope's current entry for the name is a non-function (the shadowing declaration), do not fall back to an earlier function; add a call-after-shadowing fixture. VERIFIED: traced call resolution at lines 1790–1802 into the linear scan at 1047–1056.

3. [MINOR] src/lsp.rs:1074 — `scope_plain_binding` returns the scope entry (`if bindings[first].lexical { return Some(first) }`), and `declare_fresh` makes a method the scope entry when no earlier same-name plain binding exists (line 528, `scope.entry(name).or_insert(id)`). Consequently a bare non-call read of a method's own name inside the object body resolves to the method declaration, while the analyzer treats a bare method/field name as an unknown variable (the report's own repro `to can name()` / `give back name` reports `Unknown variable 'name'`). The existing object-field test uses a differently named method, so this shape is untested. Fix: keep method bindings out of the plain-name lookup (or mark them call-only) so only a real lexical variable/function answers a bare read; add a `to can name()`/`give back name` navigation test. Not run — traced; a fixture asserting the opposite would settle it.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
